    "body": "pulse task #2684 \u00b7 generation 1.\n\n<!-- pulse-operation:3ef2f68830e20bd5dca7178be9b1fe56 -->\n\nassigned to @PORTALSURFER. the frozen task plan is retained in pulse and copied below.\n\n# Pulse task #2684: W13: Build delay, modulation, reverb and looper device families on shared time DSP\n\nOperation: 3ef2f68830e20bd5dca7178be9b1fe56\nAssignee: PORTALSURFER (Pulse #1, GitHub ID 41680373)\n\nPart of Hex native device roadmap #2661, goal 3, version 0.1. Astra assessment: 17 October 2026.\n\nGoal: deliver complete time-based devices with correct long-lived history and musical clocks.\n\nDeliverables:\n- Shared delay rings/interpolation, taps, smooth time changes, tempo-sync subdivisions, phase/retrigger, feedback/filtering, freeze, diffusion/allpass and deterministic modulation. Select per-device transition laws explicitly; pitch-changing versus crossfade time edits are behavior, not implementation accidents.\n- Focused device order: Delay -> Filter Delay/Chorus-Ensemble/Phaser-Flanger -> Reverb/Echo -> Beat Repeat/Grain Delay -> Hybrid Reverb/Looper. Convolution/FFT/pitch primitives and assets arrive from W17/W10; Looper recording integrates canonical recording ownership.\n- Each device implements its full state/mode/control inventory and proper panel, not one generic delay with renamed headers. Reuse W07 graphs/tempo controls and W09 ports where required.\n- Specify long/infinite tails, feedback safety, freeze release, bypass warm/freeze, seek/chase/loop/tempo changes, sample-rate reconstruction, live delay changes and removal/cancellation. No unbounded preroll or forced giant-tail render.\n- Looper needs bounded capture/overdub, Undo/clear/reverse, synchronization and asset/persistence policy; do not keep an unbounded callback Vec or silently lose captured audio.\n\nAcceptance: impulse/tap/feedback and modulation oracles, finite-tail/quiescence/freeze tests, uneven-block/tempo partitioning, held-history retarget and old-chain retirement all pass. Export/capture handles tails and cancellation deliberately; long buffers fit W04 budgets and native W20 deadlines. Musical mode audits and user listening complete each device.\n\nSources: existing lattice tail/clock/history contracts and official audio reference. W04's large-state spike precedes production adoption; #2388/#2389 retain listening/export qualification ownership.\n\nPlanning workstream: split implementation into focused PRs as work begins. Related canonical tickets: #2108, #2388, #2389. Preserve their current scope, versions, status and PR history. Dependency edges order the enabling contracts; downstream production proof is qualified incrementally under W20.\n\ncreated by pulse shared PR intake/start task. builds, checks and merging follow the repository's own instructions.",
